1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:02,734
On the second of October,
twenty twenty-six,

2
00:00:02,801 --> 00:00:06,640
Nike's stock touched thirty-one
dollars and ninety-seven cents.

3
00:00:07,070 --> 00:00:09,314
An intraday low not
seen in thirteen years.

4
00:00:09,381 --> 00:00:12,390
So how did the most famous
brand in sport fall this far?

5
00:00:12,820 --> 00:00:14,951
This is the story of how two brothers,

6
00:00:15,018 --> 00:00:18,200
one rookie and one very
expensive decision got us here.

7
00:00:18,630 --> 00:00:20,001
Three weeks after that low,

8
00:00:20,068 --> 00:00:23,210
Adidas reported the most
profitable quarter in its history.

9
00:00:23,640 --> 00:00:25,673
Everyone says Adidas finally beat Nike.

10
00:00:25,740 --> 00:00:28,740
But that quarter, Adidas's
shoe sales didn't grow at all.

11
00:00:29,160 --> 00:00:32,693
So that's not the real story. Nike
took its own shoes off the shelf.

12
00:00:32,760 --> 00:00:35,450
And Adidas sold the shoes it already had.

13
00:00:35,890 --> 00:00:39,220
Remember the word shelf.
It decides the ending.

14
00:00:39,653 --> 00:00:43,733
It starts in a small Bavarian town,
Herzogenaurach, in nineteen twenty-four.

15
00:00:44,163 --> 00:00:47,983
Two brothers, Adi and Rudolf Dassler,
register a shoe factory together.

16
00:00:48,413 --> 00:00:52,573
In nineteen thirty-six, Jesse Owens wins
four Olympic gold medals in their spikes.

17
00:00:52,993 --> 00:00:55,573
Then, in nineteen forty-eight,
the brothers split.

18
00:00:56,003 --> 00:00:58,166
Rudolf founds the company
that becomes Puma.

19
00:00:58,233 --> 00:01:00,446
Adi registers his own
in nineteen forty-nine,

20
00:01:00,513 --> 00:01:02,793
with forty-seven employees
and three stripes.

21
00:01:03,223 --> 00:01:06,413
The town splits with them,
one factory on each side of the river.

22
00:01:06,843 --> 00:01:10,984
In nineteen sixty-four, in Oregon, a
young runner, Phil Knight, and his coach,

23
00:01:11,051 --> 00:01:13,963
Bill Bowerman, start importing
Japanese running shoes.

24
00:01:14,393 --> 00:01:16,313
In nineteen seventy-one they become Nike.

25
00:01:16,380 --> 00:01:19,093
A design student draws the
logo for thirty-five dollars.

26
00:01:19,523 --> 00:01:22,593
Bowerman makes a new sole by
pouring rubber into a waffle iron.

27
00:01:23,023 --> 00:01:26,413
So Adidas was the giant.
Nike was the upstart.

28
00:01:26,853 --> 00:01:29,964
By nineteen eighty-four,
Adidas was the bigger company,

29
00:01:30,031 --> 00:01:32,573
and the rookie Michael
Jordan wanted Adidas.

30
00:01:33,003 --> 00:01:34,636
But Adidas didn't match Nike's offer.

31
00:01:34,703 --> 00:01:38,663
Nike gave him five years, two and a
half million dollars, and his own shoe.

32
00:01:39,103 --> 00:01:42,984
That October, Jordan wore a black
and red Nike in a preseason game,

33
00:01:43,051 --> 00:01:44,583
and the league flagged it.

34
00:01:45,003 --> 00:01:47,414
The legend says Nike
paid a fine every game.

35
00:01:47,481 --> 00:01:49,733
ESPN found no evidence it ever paid one.

36
00:01:50,163 --> 00:01:53,116
It wasn't even the Air Jordan. It
was a shoe called the Air Ship.

37
00:01:53,183 --> 00:01:55,393
So Nike turned the warning
into an ad: banned.

38
00:01:55,823 --> 00:01:59,713
The Air Jordan went on sale in nineteen
eighty-five, at sixty-five dollars.

39
00:02:00,143 --> 00:02:03,643
By the end of that year, its sales had
passed one hundred million dollars.

40
00:02:04,063 --> 00:02:06,911
That was Nike's real formula:
a shoe people lined up for,

41
00:02:06,978 --> 00:02:08,103
on shelves everywhere.

42
00:02:08,533 --> 00:02:09,743
Remember that shelf.

43
00:02:10,720 --> 00:02:14,390
For thirty-five years, that formula
worked. Then, in June twenty twenty,

44
00:02:14,457 --> 00:02:16,933
Nike's chief executive,
John Donahoe, changed it.

45
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:19,180
He called it Consumer Direct Acceleration.

46
00:02:19,610 --> 00:02:23,500
The plan: sell through Nike's own app
and stores, and cut out the middlemen.

47
00:02:23,930 --> 00:02:27,260
Nike walked away from Belk,
Dillard's, Zappos and other stores.

48
00:02:27,680 --> 00:02:29,199
By fiscal twenty twenty-four,

49
00:02:29,266 --> 00:02:32,710
direct sales were about forty-four
percent of Nike brand sales.

50
00:02:33,140 --> 00:02:36,310
The margin was higher. But it
meant the shelf was shrinking.

51
00:02:36,740 --> 00:02:40,565
And the app kept selling the same
few classics: Dunks, Air Force Ones,

52
00:02:40,632 --> 00:02:41,410
Jordan retros.

53
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:46,080
A Bloomberg profile of Donahoe was
titled "The Man Who Made Nike Uncool."

54
00:02:46,510 --> 00:02:49,430
In fiscal twenty twenty-five,
revenue fell ten percent.

55
00:02:49,860 --> 00:02:52,530
Net income fell forty-four percent.

56
00:02:52,960 --> 00:02:54,547
Donahoe left. Elliott Hill,

57
00:02:54,614 --> 00:02:56,508
who started at Nike as an intern

58
00:02:56,575 --> 00:02:59,816
in nineteen eighty-eight and
retired in twenty twenty,

59
00:02:59,883 --> 00:03:01,660
came back as chief executive.

60
00:03:02,100 --> 00:03:04,440
His first job: get Nike
back on the shelves.

61
00:03:04,853 --> 00:03:08,833
But here's the part nobody saw coming.
Adidas, meanwhile, had its own disaster.

62
00:03:09,263 --> 00:03:12,773
In October twenty twenty-two,
it ended its partnership with Ye,

63
00:03:12,840 --> 00:03:14,883
the rapper once known as Kanye West.

64
00:03:15,313 --> 00:03:15,657
ADIDAS:

65
00:03:15,724 --> 00:03:19,953
adidas does not tolerate antisemitism
and any other sort of hate speech.

66
00:03:20,383 --> 00:03:22,806
It was left with a warehouse
full of Yeezy shoes.

67
00:03:22,873 --> 00:03:23,688
And a forecast:

68
00:03:23,755 --> 00:03:27,627
a seven-hundred-million-euro
operating loss in twenty twenty-three,

69
00:03:27,694 --> 00:03:29,163
if it couldn't sell them.

70
00:03:29,593 --> 00:03:32,486
The new chief executive,
Bjorn Gulden, came from Puma.

71
00:03:32,553 --> 00:03:35,005
GULDEN: The numbers speak for themselves.

72
00:03:35,072 --> 00:03:38,143
We are currently not
performing the way we should.

73
00:03:38,573 --> 00:03:42,173
Then he did something deeply unglamorous.
He sold what Adidas already had.

74
00:03:42,603 --> 00:03:48,023
The Samba. The Gazelle. The Spezial.
Old shoes, made in far bigger numbers.

75
00:03:48,453 --> 00:03:52,103
The Samba was named shoe of the year,
the brand's first since twenty fifteen.

76
00:03:52,523 --> 00:03:55,504
And instead of writing the
Yeezys off, Adidas sold them,

77
00:03:55,571 --> 00:03:57,686
for about one point four billion euros.

78
00:03:57,753 --> 00:04:00,553
It set aside two hundred and
sixty million for charity.

79
00:04:00,993 --> 00:04:04,531
Operating profit went from two
hundred and sixty-eight million euros

80
00:04:04,598 --> 00:04:08,043
in twenty twenty-three to over
two billion in twenty twenty-five.

81
00:04:08,463 --> 00:04:10,533
Adidas had climbed back onto the shelf.

82
00:04:10,953 --> 00:04:14,733
So who actually won twenty
twenty-six? First came the World Cup.

83
00:04:15,163 --> 00:04:17,098
Nike's six-minute film, Rip the Script,

84
00:04:17,165 --> 00:04:21,006
passed sixty-six million YouTube views
in under a week, the press reported.

85
00:04:21,073 --> 00:04:22,903
Adidas's ad stayed under seven million.

86
00:04:23,333 --> 00:04:27,263
Nike won the attention. Then
it reported the quarter.

87
00:04:27,693 --> 00:04:32,373
Revenue fell four percent. In
Greater China, it fell twenty-six.

88
00:04:32,803 --> 00:04:36,793
Nike told investors to expect revenue
to fall again this fiscal year.

89
00:04:37,223 --> 00:04:39,853
The next day, the stock
hit its thirteen-year low.

90
00:04:40,283 --> 00:04:43,076
Nike's chief executive,
Elliott Hill, put it this way.

91
00:04:43,143 --> 00:04:43,378
HILL:

92
00:04:43,445 --> 00:04:46,572
Our Nike performance business
is not yet large enough

93
00:04:46,639 --> 00:04:49,948
to offset the pressure we are
seeing in Nike sportswear,

94
00:04:50,015 --> 00:04:51,943
Jordan brand, and Greater China.

95
00:04:52,373 --> 00:04:53,313
Three weeks later,

96
00:04:53,380 --> 00:04:57,678
Adidas made just over a billion euros
of operating profit in a single quarter,

97
00:04:57,745 --> 00:04:59,423
the first time in its history.

98
00:04:59,853 --> 00:05:03,373
About two hundred million euros
of it was a one-off tariff refund.

99
00:05:03,793 --> 00:05:07,593
And its footwear sales were flat.
Adidas grew on clothing and sports gear.

100
00:05:08,023 --> 00:05:11,753
So Adidas didn't beat Nike at shoes.
Nike had stepped off the shelf.

101
00:05:12,187 --> 00:05:15,002
Today, wholesale is Nike's
growing channel again:

102
00:05:15,069 --> 00:05:18,657
sixty-one percent of brand sales
in fiscal twenty twenty-six.

103
00:05:19,087 --> 00:05:21,583
The company behind the
world's most famous shoe

104
00:05:21,650 --> 00:05:23,887
forgot that people have to find it first.

105
00:05:24,307 --> 00:05:27,524
And the company facing a
seven-hundred-million-euro loss

106
00:05:27,591 --> 00:05:29,937
was saved by shoes from its own archive.

107
00:05:30,367 --> 00:05:33,286
Here's the lesson. The
shelf is the strategy.

108
00:05:33,353 --> 00:05:36,007
Where you sell is part of what you sell.

109
00:05:36,437 --> 00:05:39,897
Nike shows investors its new plan
on the seventeenth of November.

110
00:05:40,327 --> 00:05:45,067
The question is whether a company can
win back a shelf it walked away from.

111
00:05:45,497 --> 00:05:48,752
So remember that shelf
from nineteen eighty-five,

112
00:05:48,819 --> 00:05:52,006
because Nike built an
empire on being everywhere

113
00:05:52,073 --> 00:05:54,582
and then decided it didn't need to be,

114
00:05:54,649 --> 00:05:58,100
while Adidas simply showed
up wherever people shop.

115
00:05:58,167 --> 00:06:02,463
If Nike gets its shoes back on those
shelves, this was a bad two years,

116
00:06:02,530 --> 00:06:07,017
and if it doesn't, the brothers' old
rival may be the one still standing.

117
00:06:07,447 --> 00:06:11,027
Tell us in the comments:
the new Nike, or the old Samba?

